                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "So this is a graphically simple puzzle game, where you must attempt to use various ramps and blocks (both on your side of reality and reflected on the shadow side) to access the portals and move to the next set of levels/worlds.\r\n\r\nThe animations/artwork are basic but effective, the music is rather repetitive but not annoyingly so, and up until the very last world the puzzles are increasingly complicated without being frustratingly difficult, i.e. you will eventually be able to solve them without ever even thinking about using a walkthrough.  \r\n\r\nThe last world on the other hand, can go fellate decomposing dingos.  Is it incredibly hard?  Well, not really, but in comparison to everything that has come before it, it was rather frustrating (yes, totally a skill/brain issue) and I had to look things up a few times to make sure I wasn't completely out to lunch after the ninth or tenth time I tried to create a solution.\r\n\r\nSome people (especially those who have good spatial/logical skills and who can visualize things three or four steps in the future) will find the last world a decent challenge.  I merely found it annoyingly frustrating.  Obviously your mileage will vary.  Again, totally a skill issue, but when a game stops being fun.......well, then it's harder to look at it with affection.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, the achievements for completing the entire game in under three hours, and the one for doing EVERY SINGLE LEVEL again, but with the orientations of the world and protagonist reversed can go die in a fire.  Not gonna happen.\r\n\r\nAnyway, 90% of the game is chock full of enjoyable puzzling, so ultimately recommended.",
